.SH A) OVERVIEW
file creation (file-steps) are the creation and loading of files, and possibly
stdin or ncurses.  these are life-savers with programs as the data is then
baked into the unit-test rather than trying to corral and maintain many
separate input files.

.SH B) TECHNICAL
file-steps are simply turned into c-language code for the manipulation of files
or interactive input.

.SH C) EXAMPLE
file-steps have four fields.  the leading spaces are changed to spacers (·)
in this manual for clarity.

   verb------- § desc § -func- § ---parms------------------------------------ §
   ·····file   § new  § - - -  § /root/test.gyges                             §
   ·····append § add  § - - -  § #!/usr/bin/gyges                             §
   ·····appvis § add  § - - -  § cell    field     data                       §
   ·····load   § char § stdin  § text placed into standard input file (0)     §

APPEND and APPVIS work identically, but APPEND translates specific special
characters into control characters (such as return or space) and APPVIS leaves
them visible.  LOAD also translates them.

.SH D) VERB
the verbs FILE, APPEND, APPVIS, and LOAD are is expected to be indented five
(5) characters off-setting it from COND.  the verb field are is twelve (12)
characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.

   verb------- § desc § -func- § ---parms---------------------------------- §
   ·····file   § ·    § ·      § ·                                          §
   ·····append § ·    § ·      § ·                                          §
   ·····appvis § ·    § ·      § ·                                          §
   ·····load   § ·    § ·      § ·                                          §

.SH E) DESCRIPTION
it is very unhelpful to leave off a description as unit-testing is almost
invariably detailed, complicated, and extensive.  descriptions are a maximum
of thirty-five (35) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.
legal characters are letters, numbers, and most punctuation.

since descriptions are demonstrated elsewhere, i have truncated them greatly
to fit everything in the available space ;)

   verb------- § desc § -func- § ---parms---------------------------------- §
   ·····file   § new  § ·      § ·                                          §
   ·····append § add  § ·      § ·                                          §
   ·····appvis § add  § ·      § ·                                          §
   ·····load   § char § ·      § ·                                          §

.SH F) FUNCTION
the third field is left blank, except in the case of LOAD.  for LOAD, the
options are STDIN or NCURSES.  STDIN clears and loads data into file (0).
NCURSES is essentially the same, but the input stream is in curses.  in both
cases, if mulitple lines must be added, load them in reverse order (LIFO).

   verb------- § desc § -func- § ---parms---------------------------------- §
   ·····file   § ·    § - - -  § ·                                          §
   ·····append § ·    § - - -  § ·                                          §
   ·····appvis § ·    § - - -  § ·                                          §
   ·····load   § ·    § stdin  § ·                                          §

.SH G) PARAMS
the fourth field is the actual data to load, most things are just typed as
you which to load them, except FILE where the data is the file name.  but,
intentional leading/trailing spaces should be passed as spacers (·) as the
input field is trimmed as the field is read.

   verb------- § desc § -func- § ---parms---------------------------------- §
   ·····file   § new  § - - -  § /root/test.gyges                           §
   ·····append § add  § - - -  § #!/usr/bin/gyges                           §
   ·····appvis § add  § - - -  § cell    field     data                     §
   ·····load   § char § stdin  § text placed into standard input file (0)   §

given the way koios parses the data, some characters must use their visible
counter-part in order to be passed to the file.  remember APPVIS leaves
visible version in input, but APPEND and LOAD convert them to control chars.

   ·       ascii (0x20) space             used in leading/trailing spaces
   ¦       ascii (0x0a) linefeed/return   using \\\\n is messy
   ¥       ascii (0x1b) escape            using \\\\e is messy
   ¶       ascii (0x22) double quote      using \\\\" is messy
   µ       ascii (0x5c) backslash         using \\\\ is messy
   §       ascii (0x1c) field separator
   ¨       ascii (0x1d) group separator

.SH H) OUTPUT
when the unit-test is run, each file-step generates small block of output.
the output says it is the 4th step in the condition (ad) and is generated by
line 39015 in the source code.  everything else is a reflection of input values.
this line will also be "green" and say PASS is it passed or be "red" and say
FAIL if it failed for fast visual scanning.

  ···ad) PASS   : å·····fghiæ read for writing ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·º39015»
  ·······file   :  41å/tmp/little.small_sample_project/little.hæ

  ···ae) PASS   : å·····fghiæ add a record  ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·· ·º39018»
  ·······append :  22åchar ANY__free (void);æ

NOTE: if the line number is [00555] is is in a specific unit test source file.
if like å00555æ it is in a shared file -- unit_data.unit or unit_wide.unit.
